    "## 17. Analysis and Interpretation\n",
    "\n",
    "A thorough, multi-dimensional analytical evaluation of the complete simple pendulum experimental dataset ($N = 80$) reveals key insights into physical system behavior, measurement uncertainty, and statistical distributions:\n",
    "\n",
    "### A. Overall Experimental Behavior\n",
    "The 80 experimental measurements demonstrate high empirical conformity to Newtonian harmonic oscillator mechanics. Across all 8 lengths ($0.20\\,\\text{m}$ to $1.00\\,\\text{m}$), observed periods range from $0.8948\\,\\text{s}$ to $2.0485\\,\\text{s}$, closely bracketing the theoretical interval of $0.8971\\,\\text{s}$ to $2.0061\\,\\text{s}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "### B. Difference Between Experimental and Theoretical Values\n",
    "Differences between experimental and theoretical values are predominantly minute. The mean signed discrepancy across the entire dataset is $+0.0069\\,\\text{s}$ ($+6.9\\,\\text{ms}$), reflecting a slight positive bias consistent with human stopwatch trigger latency during oscillation endpoint detection.\n",
    "\n",
    "### C. Error Magnitude\n",
    "The Mean Absolute Error (MAE) is $0.0214\\,\\text{s}$ ($21.4\\,\\text{ms}$) with a median absolute error of $0.0152\\,\\text{s}$. The standard deviation of absolute error is $0.0267\\,\\text{s}$, indicating that typical measurement variations cluster within a very narrow $15 - 30\\,\\text{ms}$ window.\n",
    "\n",
    "### D. Error Distribution\n",
    "The percentage error distribution spans from $0.0298\\%$ (trial `EXP052`) to $10.2896\\%$ (trial `EXP055`) with a mean of $1.5243\\%$ and a median of $1.0119\\%$. The distribution is strongly right-skewed, with $77.50\\%$ of observations exhibiting less than $2.0\\%$ error.\n",
    "\n",
    "### E. Variation in Experimental Measurements\n",
    "Intra-group measurement variation remains low across all lengths. The standard deviation of experimental periods within repeated 10-trial clusters ranges between a minimum of $0.0159\\,\\text{s}$ (at $L = 0.20\\,\\text{m}$) and a maximum of $0.0533\\,\\text{s}$ (at $L = 0.70\\,\\text{m}$).\n",
    "\n",
    "### F. Relationship Between Pendulum Length and Period\n",
    "The relationship between length and time period is non-linear, monotonic, and positive, exhibiting near-perfect linear correlation in the transformed domain ($r = +0.992$). This empirically verifies the square-root power-law relation $T \\propto \\sqrt{L}$.\n",
    "\n",
    "### G. Relationship Between Length and Experimental Error\n",
    "Pearson correlation between length and percentage error is $r = -0.123$ ($p > 0.05$), demonstrating that experimental percentage error is scale-invariant and does not systematically increase or decrease as pendulum length changes.\n",
    "\n",
    "### H. Potential Outliers\n",
    "The Interquartile Range (IQR) method on percentage errors flagged exactly 5 observations ($6.25\\%$ of dataset: `EXP004`, `EXP007`, `EXP014`, `EXP038`, `EXP055`). These correspond to isolated manual timing offsets rather than systemic physical anomalies and were transparently retained.\n",
    "\n",
    "### I. Correlations Among Numerical Variables\n",
    "Bivariate correlation analysis demonstrates that physical dimensions (`Length_m`, `Theoretical_Period_s`, `Experimental_Period_s`) are almost perfectly collinear ($r \\ge 0.992$), while error metrics (`Absolute_Error_s`, `Relative_Error`, `Percentage_Error`) form an interconnected cluster ($r \\ge 0.963$).\n",
    "\n",
    "### J. Overall Experimental Consistency\n",
    "With $48.75\\%$ of trials achieving Low Error ($< 1\\%$) and $28.75\\%$ achieving Moderate Error ($1 - 2\\%$), the overall laboratory procedure demonstrates high repeatability and excellent measurement fidelity."
